<html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Feelings in interview I40</title><!--[if gte mso 9]><xml><w:WordDocument><w:View>Print</w:View></w:WordDocument></xml><![endif]--><style>body,p,li,td{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.35;color:#17130f}h1,h2,h3{font-family:Cambria,Georgia,serif;font-weight:normal;color:#8c1912}h1{font-size:20pt}h2{font-size:14pt}p.note{font-size:9pt}td,th{border:1px solid #e4dcd0;padding:3pt 5pt;font-size:9pt;vertical-align:top}table{border-collapse:collapse}</style></head><body><p style="color:#8b7f72;font-size:9pt">RUBICON REPORT</p><h1>Feelings in interview I40</h1><p class="asked"><b>Question</b> In interview I40 (man, 23, Tower Hamlets), where does the interviewee express or report their own positive, negative or mixed feelings? For each example: its valence, a short label for the emotion, what it is about, and whether he ties it in that passage to loneliness, to feeling connected, or to neither; and how many examples of each valence, and of each link, he gives. Feelings the interviewer suggests and the interviewee does not take up, feelings attributed to other people, and purely hypothetical feelings do not count.</p><p>The interviewee in I40 gives slightly more unpleasant feelings than pleasant ones: 5 passages negative (and 1 more only weakly), 4 passages positive (and 1 more only weakly) and 1 passage mixed. The unpleasant ones are about being among strangers or alone somewhere unfamiliar, and about crowding; the pleasant ones are about his local hang-out, time on his own, living in the area and, weakly, having a friend with him when lost. He ties 4 passages to loneliness, 2 passages to feeling connected (and 1 more only weakly), and 4 passages to neither (and 1 more only weakly).</p><h2 id="s-loneliness-among-strangers">Loneliness among strangers</h2>
<p>His loneliness is not about having no friends but about being where he knows no one. On the tube he says &quot;I guess I feel lonely that&#x27;s the most lonely place I can kind of think of&quot; (I40, “When I&#x27;m on the tube…”), and of Liverpool Street, &quot;It just feels lonely ‘cause there’s no one I know there, not at all&quot; (I40, “It just feels lonely ‘cause…”). There, he says, &quot;I wanna quickly get in and out of that place&quot; (I40), which he puts with the same loneliness: &quot;seems like everyone is alone as well. That’s why I feel lonely I guess over there&quot; (I40).</p>
<p>Being alone somewhere unfamiliar also makes him nervous. Split up from his friends on holiday and taking a cab on his own, &quot;I was alone by myself so made me feel quite nervous&quot; (I40, “So, the whole ride was…”). He sets this against the same situation with a friend, when &quot;I almost didn&#x27;t care&quot; (I40, “the exact same situation happened…”); that passage implies ease rather than naming it, and he names it later only in a general second person: &quot;it just makes you feel at ease&quot; (I40).</p>
<p>One of the feelings he ties to being alone is pleasant. He does not mind, and sometimes wants, time by himself in familiar places: &quot;being alone I wouldn’t say is something I don&#x27;t like…sometime I’d like to… I would like to you know just go somewhere by myself&quot; (I40, “being alone I wouldn’t say…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-crowds-and-the-city">Crowds and the city</h2>
<p>Several unpleasant feelings concern crowding, and these passages do not tie them to loneliness: dislike and awkwardness when the train is packed (I40, “Pretty crowded. Yeah I don&#x27;t…”), stress that gives way to relief &quot;when the space opens up&quot; (I40, “It’s just a bit maybe…”), and dislike of the rush at Liverpool Street, &quot;I don&#x27;t really like it&quot; (I40, “Yeah when it’s busy, crazy…”). He describes tube journeys as &quot;something you just want to get through&quot; (I40, “I don&#x27;t know it just…”), in a general second person. Against this he says &quot;I enjoy living here&quot; (I40, “I mean…I enjoy living here”).</p>
<h2 id="s-feeling-connected">Feeling connected</h2>
<p>Both firm feelings he ties to connection concern the local hang-out by the football pitch, where &quot;Almost every faces are familiar face&quot; (I40): he says he feels &quot;most socially connected&quot; there (I40, “It’s just really local to…”) and &quot;Just very, very comfortable&quot; (I40, “Just very, very comfortable”). The weak one is the ease of being lost on holiday with a friend (I40, “the exact same situation happened…”).</p>
<h2 id="s-limits">Limits</h2>
<p>Much of what he says about fear and nervousness is in a general second person about imagined situations, which the definitions leave out; counting those would add negative feelings tied to loneliness.</p><h2>How this was made</h2><h3>What was read</h3><p>All 48 documents, read in full: 24 male, 24 female.</p>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:one"><h3>Sample: One</h3></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:emotions"><h3>Coding: Emotions</h3><p class="small">one row for each passage that fits; 12 rows.</p><details><summary>The instruction a second coder would follow</summary><blockquote>Read the interview. Find every passage in which the interviewee (not the interviewer) expresses or reports a feeling of their own: a feeling they have now or have had, in their own words. Loneliness counts as a feeling. A generalised &#x27;you&#x27; or &#x27;it can feel&#x27; counts only where the interviewee ties it in that passage to their own experience. Do not code: feelings the interviewee imagines for a figure in a drawing or for people in general; purely hypothetical feelings (&#x27;I would feel&#x27;); feelings the interviewer puts forward that the interviewee only agrees to with &#x27;yeah&#x27; or &#x27;uhum&#x27;. Where the same feeling about the same thing is repeated or elaborated in follow-up answers, code it once, quoting the clearest words. Where one answer names feelings about two different things, code each. Where one answer names several feelings about the same thing, code them as one passage and name them all in the emotion label; where a later answer names a different feeling about the same thing, code that too. Code a feeling the interviewee has about another person&#x27;s situation (for example, sadness at a friend&#x27;s distress) as their own feeling, but not a feeling they say the other person has. A reported absence of feeling (&#x27;it doesn&#x27;t make me feel anything&#x27;) is not coded. For each passage, copy the words exactly and fill the columns.</blockquote></details><h4>Valence</h4><p>Whether the feeling expressed is pleasant, unpleasant or both.</p><p class="small">Weak: The feeling is not named as the interviewee&#x27;s own but carried by a generalised &#x27;you&#x27; about his own situation, or only implied (such as &#x27;I almost didn&#x27;t care&#x27;) rather than said.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Positive</th><td>A pleasant feeling: happiness, enjoyment, relief, gratitude, love, hope, ease, respect, calm acceptance.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;I feel good, happy&#x27;, &#x27;I enjoy&#x27;, &#x27;it&#x27;s alright now&#x27;.</td><td>Neutral description of an activity with no feeling named or plainly expressed.</td></tr><tr><th>Negative</th><td>An unpleasant feeling: sadness, loneliness, frustration, resentment, boredom, unease, longing, resignation, distress.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;it makes me sad&#x27;, &#x27;I feel quite lonely&#x27;, &#x27;I don&#x27;t feel okay&#x27;.</td><td>A passage that pairs an unpleasant feeling with a pleasant or accepting one about the same thing: code mixed.</td></tr><tr><th>Mixed</th><td>Pleasant and unpleasant feelings, or acceptance and hurt, expressed together about the same thing.</td><td>Statements such as &#x27;conflicting emotions&#x27; or &#x27;I&#x27;m used to it now but sometimes it can get to me&#x27;. Also an unpleasant feeling in the past and a pleasant or accepting one now about the same thing, in the same passage (&#x27;when I was a lot younger, it did affect me ... I’m content and I feel happier&#x27;).</td><td>Two feelings about different things in the same answer: code each separately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Emotion</h4><p>A short label, one to three words, for the feeling, such as loneliness, frustration, gratitude, relief; where one passage names several feelings, list each.</p><h4>Subject</h4><p>In a few words, what the feeling is about or what brings it on, as the interviewee describes it.</p><h4>Link</h4><p>Whether the interviewee, in the coded passage itself, ties the feeling to loneliness or to feeling connected.</p><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th><th>Counts</th><th>Does not count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Loneliness</th><td>The feeling is loneliness, or the interviewee ties it in the passage to loneliness, being alone, having no one, not fitting in, or being an outsider or &#x27;black sheep&#x27;.</td><td>&#x27;that sometimes makes me feel lonely&#x27;; &#x27;why do I feel like an outsider&#x27;; &#x27;I don&#x27;t mind if I don&#x27;t sit with anyone&#x27; (a feeling about being alone, whatever its valence). A feeling about being or working alone counts whatever its valence, including relief or enjoyment at working alone rather than with others (&#x27;much less anxious than I was when I work within a team&#x27;).</td><td>A feeling about a situation the interviewee elsewhere calls lonely, where this passage does not make the tie; loneliness the interviewee attributes to other people. Enjoyment of freedom or independence where the passage does not speak of being alone or without other people (code neither).</td></tr><tr><th>Connection</th><td>The interviewee ties the feeling in the passage to feeling connected, belonging, being accepted or understood, or closeness and enjoyment with other people.</td><td>&#x27;a sense of belongingness&#x27;; &#x27;I felt like they understood me&#x27;; &#x27;I love them and trust them with everything&#x27;.</td><td>Enjoyment of a place, activity or freedom with no other people named as the source of the feeling.</td></tr><tr><th>Neither</th><td>The passage ties the feeling to neither loneliness nor connection.</td><td>Anger at a service, guilt about family contact, anxiety about a routine, where the passage names no tie.</td><td>Any passage that fits loneliness or connection.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:by_valence"><h3>Counting: Rows by valence <span class="small">(by_valence)</span></h3><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:by_valence"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">3 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Valence</th><th>Rows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Negative</td><td>5 of None</td></tr><tr><td>Positive</td><td>4 of None</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed</td><td>1 of None</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:by_link"><h3>Counting: Rows by link <span class="small">(by_link)</span></h3><div class="rb-block" data-node="asset:by_link"><details><summary>The table <span class="small">3 cells</span></summary><div class="scroll"><table class="codebook"><thead><tr><th>Link</th><th>Rows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Loneliness</td><td>4 of None</td></tr><tr><td>Neither</td><td>4 of None</td></tr><tr><td>Connection</td><td>2 of None</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:answer"><h3>Writing the answer</h3><details><summary>The instruction the answer was written to</summary><blockquote>Set out what he feels, about what, with the counts by valence and by link, and which feelings he ties to loneliness and which to feeling connected.</blockquote></details></section>
<h3>The checks code ran</h3><ul><li>12 of 12 quotations found word for word in their document.</li><li>10 numbers in the answer, every one counted by code; 0 written by hand.</li><li>0 citations to passages that do not exist; 0 quotations not in the passage they cite.</li></ul>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:second_coding"><h3>Second coding</h3><p>A second coder coded the documents afresh to the same definitions, without seeing the first coding.</p></section>
<section class="rb-block" data-node="step:check"><h3>The check</h3><p>A fresh reader ruled on every place the two codings differed, and checked every sentence and quotation of the answer against the documents, correcting the coding and the answer where they were wrong. The answer above is the corrected one, recounted after the check.</p><p class="small">- Disagreements ruled: 2 (recode/disagreements.md lists none at document level, since there is one document; these are the two passages the first coder coded and the second did not, from a row-by-row comparison of coded/emotions.json, 13 rows, with recode/coded/emotions.json, 11 rows). For the rows: 1. For the second coder: 1. Unsettled: 0.
- Points in recode/unclear.md reviewed: 3; none changes a row.
- Rows changed: 1 (1 removed, 0 added, 0 edited). emotions now has 12 rows.
- Definitions changed in workflow.json: 0.
- Sentences in answer.md changed: 13 (11 rewritten or corrected, 1 added, 1 cut).</p></section>
<h3>The record</h3><p>Code drew this report from the run's zip, in-interview-i40-man-23-tower-hamlets-where-does-2026-10-08-d0ee0727.zip, with Rubicon 0.1.0+bfb9b34fd622. The zip's fingerprint is <code class="fingerprint">d0ee0727ae73bd50d92f8d9af1671ff83a0fb800f18c38e892f9e640d624ee70</code>. It is the SHA-256 of the zip's SHA256SUMS file, which lists the SHA-256 of every other file in the zip, so changing any file breaks the match. To check that the zip is the one this report was drawn from, unzip it, run <code>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS</code>, then <code>sha256sum SHA256SUMS</code>, and compare the result with the fingerprint above. Rubicon's own code, which is public, checks the rest: <code>python verify.py &lt;report&gt; &lt;zip&gt;</code> recounts every number from the coded passages in the zip, draws the report again from the zip and says whether it matches this one exactly.</p><h2>Passages cited</h2><p class="note">I40: "When I&#x27;m on the tube, you don&#x27;t know anyone when you’re on the tube. So that’s kind of way I guess I feel lonely that&#x27;s the most lonely place I can kind of think of."</p><p class="note">I40: "It just feels lonely ‘cause there’s no one I know there, not at all"</p><p class="note">I40: "So, the whole ride was just nervous. But I end up in the right place. So, it just makes you uneasy I guess the whole time and I was alone by myself so made me feel quite nervous."</p><p class="note">I40: "the exact same situation happened to me in another holiday with my mate I was having one person with me, and I was complete I almost didn&#x27;t care"</p><p class="note">I40: "being alone I wouldn’t say is something I don&#x27;t like…sometime I’d like to… I would like to you know just go somewhere by myself"</p><p class="note">I40: "Pretty crowded. Yeah I don&#x27;t like that I hate…when it gets really crowded everyone is just in everyone’s personal space…you know…you just feel really awkward I guess."</p><p class="note">I40: "It’s just a bit maybe a bit stressful like again I was just like I would like to get over and get done quickly when the space opens up the train I feel like relieved now"</p><p class="note">I40: "Yeah when it’s busy, crazy like that…I don&#x27;t really like it."</p><p class="note">I40: "I don&#x27;t know it just feels like just feel something you dealing with…something you just want to get through"</p><p class="note">I40: "I mean…I enjoy living here."</p><p class="note">I40: "It’s just really local to everyone that&#x27;s what I feel yeah…yeah feel most socially connected."</p><p class="note">I40: "Just very, very comfortable."</p><h2>What next</h2><p><b>For the evaluator:</b> Treat this report as a draft. Before it is final, make sense of it with the people it concerns, such as programme staff and participants: whether the findings ring true, what they leave out, and what to do about them. You can then use the Rubicon plugin to work their responses back into a revised answer.</p><p><b>For evaluation commissioners:</b> Parallel to this report is a zip file (in-interview-i40-man-23-tower-hamlets-where-does-2026-10-08-d0ee0727.zip), saved in the same folder, which contains the whole run: the documents, the coded passages and the workflow that recounts every number. Its fingerprint, under How this was made, ties it to this report: change a file in either and they no longer match. With it, anyone can check every number against the coded passages, and code the documents again to the same definitions without Rubicon or any other particular software. A fresh coding will not reproduce this report word for word, because AI models are unpredictable, but it should come close.</p><p>The Rubicon plugin for Claude is provided for free by Causal Map Ltd. Causal Map also runs workshops and consultancy on analysing qualitative evidence for evaluation: <a href="https://causalmap.app/contact/?utm_source=rubicon-plugin&amp;utm_medium=report">get in touch</a>, or follow <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/causalmap/">Causal Map on LinkedIn</a>. Coming soon: continue your Rubicon work at the Rubicon website.</p></body></html>